  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nThe forecast becomes more uncertain heading into this weekend, \nbut the general model consensus is showing the subtropical ridge \nover the southeast U.S. retrograding west into the Southern \nPlains. The western periphery of the ridge will likely nose into \neastern AZ on Saturday. If this occurs, then the subtropical jet \nwill likely shift farther west, setting up better moisture \nadvection over SoCal and western AZ. This scenario is currently \nbeing reflected within the NBM showing a westward progression of \nPoPS from southcentral AZ early Saturday into southwest AZ and \nsoutheast CA late Saturday into Sunday. Due to an overall increase\nin moisture and extensive cloud cover this weekend, it is likely \nwe will see lower instability which could limit convection and \nkeep the threat of heavy rainfall at a minimum in our forecast \nregion with better prospects of heavy rain and flash flooding over\nSoCal and S NV. There is still plenty of time for things to \nchange and we will likely have to wait until we are within the \nhigh-res window for this weekend's forecast to become more clear.\n\nSimilar to rain chances, there is also a high uncertainty in the \ntemperature forecast by this weekend as indicated by large (5-8 \ndegree) spreads within the NBM inner quartile range, especially on \nSaturday. This is likely due to the strength of the ridge and how \nmuch cloud cover will occur over the region. It is likely that \nSaturday will be the coolest day with highs in the lower deserts \nmainly in the mid to upper 90s. However, as ridging aloft causes \nan overall increase in mid lvl hghts/thickness, temperatures may \nwarm to near or even slightly above normal from Sunday into early\nnext week.",
